\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:8.3.3}
Let $(X,\mathcal{A},\mu)$ be a measure space, let $\Omega\in\mathcal{A}$, and let $f,g:\Omega\to[0,\infty]$ be $\mathcal{A}$-measurable. Define the integral over $\Omega$ by $\int_\Omega f\,d\mu:=\int_X f\chi_\Omega\,d\mu$. If $f(x) \leq g(x)$ for all $x\in\Omega$, then $\int_{\Omega} f\,d\mu \leq \int_{\Omega} g\,d\mu$.


\end{proposition}

proof:
\begin{proof}
\label{prf:prop:8.3.3}
Extend $f$ and $g$ to $X$ by $\tilde f:=f\chi_\Omega$ and $\tilde g:=g\chi_\Omega$. Then $\tilde f,\tilde g:X\to[0,\infty]$ are measurable and $\int_\Omega f\,d\mu=\int_X \tilde f\,d\mu$, $\int_\Omega g\,d\mu=\int_X \tilde g\,d\mu$.
 If $f\le g$ on $\Omega$, then $\tilde f\le \tilde g$ on $X$. For non-negative integrals, monotonicity holds: for every non-negative simple $\varphi\le\tilde f$ one has $\varphi\le\tilde g$, hence $\int_X\varphi\,d\mu\le\int_X\tilde g\,d\mu$. Taking the supremum over such $\varphi$ yields $\int_X\tilde f\,d\mu\le\int_X\tilde g\,d\mu$, i.e. $\int_\Omega f\,d\mu\le\int_\Omega g\,d\mu$.

\end{proof}

