\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:8.3.4}
Let $(X,\mathcal{A},\mu)$ be a measure space, let $\Omega\in\mathcal{A}$, and let $f,g:\Omega\to[0,\infty]$ be $\mathcal{A}$-measurable. Define the integral over $\Omega$ by $\int_\Omega f\,d\mu:=\int_X f\chi_\Omega\,d\mu$.If $f(x) = g(x)$ for $\mu$-almost every $x\in\Omega$, then $\int_{\Omega} f\,d\mu = \int_{\Omega} g\,d\mu$.

\end{proposition}

proof:
\begin{proof}
\label{prf:prop:8.3.4}
Extend $f$ and $g$ to $X$ by $\tilde f:=f\chi_\Omega$ and $\tilde g:=g\chi_\Omega$. Then $\tilde f,\tilde g:X\to[0,\infty]$ are measurable and $\int_\Omega f\,d\mu=\int_X \tilde f\,d\mu$, $\int_\Omega g\,d\mu=\int_X \tilde g\,d\mu$.

If $f=g$ $\mu$-a.e. on $\Omega$, then $\tilde f=\tilde g$ $\mu$-a.e. on $X$. Set $h:=\max\{\tilde f,\tilde g\}$. Then $\tilde f\le h$ and $\tilde g\le h$, and also $h\le \tilde f$ $\mu$-a.e. and $h\le \tilde g$ $\mu$-a.e. Applying (c) to the pointwise inequalities on a full-measure set gives $\int_X \tilde f\,d\mu\le\int_X h\,d\mu\le\int_X \tilde f\,d\mu$ and similarly with $\tilde g$, hence $\int_X\tilde f\,d\mu=\int_X\tilde g\,d\mu$. Therefore $\int_\Omega f\,d\mu=\int_\Omega g\,d\mu$.


\end{proof}

